[Amber Ferenz](/blog/amber-ferenz) went up in 2021 as a Hugo site: a quiet place for their compositions, scores, and videos. That post is still the launch story, this post is about switching the generator to [Zola](https://www.getzola.org/).

The site is still static. It now has search, and the works can be browsed with tags. Changing to Zola did not add anything to the site; it builds the same site 5x faster.

## Hugo is Great, Golang isn't my Vibe

Hugo is fast and opinionated. The part I kept bumping into was Go. When I wanted to extend the build, I was in a language I do not vibe with. I also did not want to keep up with Hugo's version updates; this doesn't have to be done because if whatever version built your site, it can be used at that version. But I like to keep up these things in case I get so far behind that it becomes a much heavier lift to migrate over multiple breaking changes.

Zola does everything I need for every Hugo based site I've made. Its templates are [Tera](https://keats.github.io/tera/), which is Jinja-style: loops, conditionals, and a syntax I'm already more familiar with. I struggled with the shortcode partial boundary in Hugo, feeling like I should be able to use the same 1 source file but no, it didn't work that way. So fine, maybe I misused shortcodes or partials. I just put what I needed where it was supposed to go, partials for templates, shortcodes for content. That they were suspicously similar things to me, but not the same.

Zola is written in Rust, and I do not have to write Rust to use it. Templates, Markdown, and taxonomies cover the site. Hugo required use of Golang. That Zola builds even faster makes this a win-win upgrade with no serious downside.

Tailwind CSS was a little slow before but Rust based v4 is now blazingly fast. Same for Vite, largely because Rust allowed the tool to no longer double-bundle. Build times are at least 3x faster. I have become much more comfortable with Rust after building an API server for BidSquare. That write-up is still coming.

## A fifth of the build, and then the rest of CI

Zola builds the site in roughly a fifth of the time Hugo took. I like that number. It is also a small slice of the real wait. For these sites with &lt;99 pages Hugo build: &gt;600ms and Zola &lt;200ms.

The GitHub Action that ships the site still spends most of its time on the other steps: dependencies, CSS, JavaScript, the search index, deploy. Those take about as long as they did before. The generator got quicker. End to end, the pipeline feels about the same.

## Search and tags stay static

Search is [Pagefind](https://pagefind.app/), run against the HTML after Zola finishes. Tags are a Zola taxonomy. There is no app server behind the works list. The result is still a folder of files, with search and tags on top.

## James Foulkes

[jamesfoulkes.com](https://jamesfoulkes.com) was the same kind of move, for the same reasons.

The public site did not need to change. Same pages, same URLs, same Tailwind styling, same Firebase hosting. An old link still lands on the same path.

What changed is the layer I edit. In Zola 0.23 a component is just a template call inside Markdown, so the shortcode layer is gone and that thing that used to be suspiciously similar? Now they're just a Template.
